<h2>Why Fire Safety Is Non-Negotiable in Houses In Multiple Occupation</h2>
<p>Houses in Multiple Occupation present a higher fire risk than single-family homes. More occupants mean more potential ignition sources, more people to evacuate, and greater complexity in ensuring everyone can escape safely. The law reflects this — House In Multiple Occupation landlords face significantly stricter fire safety requirements than standard rental landlords, and non-compliance can result in prosecution, unlimited fines, and loss of licence.</p>

<h2>Fire Risk Assessment</h2>
<p>A formal fire risk assessment is a legal requirement for all Houses In Multiple Occupation. It must be carried out by a competent person and reviewed regularly — or whenever significant changes are made to the property. The assessment identifies hazards, evaluates the risk to occupants, and sets out the measures needed to reduce that risk to an acceptable level. A copy must be kept on the premises and made available to residents and local authorities on request.</p>

<img src="/public/assets/images/compilance-services/fire.png" alt="Fire Safety Compliance">

<h2>Fire Detection Systems</h2>
<p>At minimum, Houses In Multiple Occupation require interlinked smoke alarms on every floor and in every habitable room, plus heat detectors in kitchens. Larger or higher-risk properties may require a full Grade A automatic fire detection system with a central control panel. All alarms must be tested regularly, and records of testing must be maintained. A Fire Detection Certificate from a qualified engineer provides documented proof of compliance.</p>

<h2>Emergency Lighting</h2>
<p>Emergency lighting ensures that escape routes remain visible during a power failure. In multi-storey Houses In Multiple Occupation especially, battery-backed emergency lighting in hallways and stairwells is typically required. An Emergency Lighting Certificate confirms the system has been installed and tested to the required standard.</p>

<h2>Fire Doors and Escape Routes</h2>
<p>Approved fire doors with self-closing devices must be fitted to all rooms opening onto escape routes. These doors are rated to contain fire for 30 minutes, giving occupants time to evacuate safely. Escape routes must be kept clear at all times — this is one of the most commonly failed checks during council inspections.</p>

<h2>Your Fire Safety Compliance Checklist</h2>
<p>Use this checklist to ensure your House In Multiple Occupation meets the minimum fire safety standards: completed and up-to-date fire risk assessment; interlinked smoke detectors on every floor and in all rooms; heat detector in the kitchen; emergency lighting in escape routes; fire doors with self-closing devices on all relevant rooms; fire extinguishers on each floor; clear and unobstructed escape routes; fire safety information provided to all tenants on move-in. Resource Housing manages all of these requirements on behalf of landlords in our portfolio — contact us to find out more.</p>
